========================================================================================
(2) PROTH TRIANGLE (shear-1) HEADLINES
========================================================================================
  rows m=0..5: [[1], [2, 1], [3, 3, 1], [4, 5, 5, 1], [5, 7, 9, 9, 1], [6, 9, 13, 17, 17, 1]]
  row sums == 2^(m+1)-1 for m<=20: True (proof: sum n*2^x over x+n=m is A000295(m+1) = 2^(m+1)-m-2; +(m+1) ones)
  collision law n(2^d-1)=d, all solutions n,d>=1: [(1, 1)] -> UNIQUE (n=1,d=1): every row m>=2 holds the FERMAT number 2^(m-1)+1 exactly twice
  row products (m, value, perfect square?): [(0, 1, True), (1, 2, False), (2, 9, True), (3, 100, True), (4, 2835, False), (5, 202878, False), (6, 35637525, False), (7, '~2^33', False), (8, '~2^43', False), (9, '~2^54', False), (10, '~2^67', False), (11, '~2^80', False), (12, '~2^95', False)]
  -> squares ONLY at m=2 (9=3^2) and m=3 (100=10^2); Fermat square (2^(m-1)+1)^2 divides every row product
  log2(row product)/C(m,2) -> [(6, 1.6725), (10, 1.4915), (14, 1.4018), (18, 1.3443), (22, 1.3033)] -> 1: PRODUCTS live on the TOURNAMENT scale 2^C(m,2);
     sums live on the subset scale 2^m.  Sum counts elements; product counts orientations.
  primes per shear-1 row (m=2..14) [Proth-prime census]: [2, 2, 2, 3, 3, 1, 2, 5, 4, 4, 5, 3, 1]

========================================================================================
(3) SHEAR-2 = THE FIBONACCI MOVE: the growth dial
========================================================================================
  proth shear-2 sums: [1, 2, 4, 7, 11, 18, 26, 41, 57, 88, 120, 183]
  minimal recurrence: a(m) = (1)a(m-1) + (3)a(m-2) + (-3)a(m-3) + (-2)a(m-4) + (2)a(m-5)
  closed form: even m=2t: 2^(t+2)-t-3; odd m=2t+1: 3*2^(t+1)-t-4; growth (sqrt2)^m: True
  char poly (x^2-2)(x-1)^2(x+1): roots +-sqrt2 (two-phase), 1 (double), -1
  GROWTH DIAL: Pascal shear-s -> Pisot root of x^s = x^(s-1)+1 (2, phi, Narayana-cows...);
               Proth  shear-s -> 2^(1/s) exactly (2, sqrt2, cbrt2...): exponential grids dial RADICALS,
               combinatorial grids dial PISOT numbers.  Both -> 1 as s -> infinity.
  charm: proth shear-2 sum at m=11 = 183 (= Phi_6(14) = the deep well denominator, charm-grade)

========================================================================================
(4) THE MUSEUM OF IMPERSONATIONS (low-order jets colliding -- the session theme)
========================================================================================
  A. proth shear-2 sums vs lazy caterer 1+T(m): [1, 2, 4, 7, 11, 18, 26, 41] vs [1, 2, 4, 7, 11, 16, 22, 29] -> split at m=5 (18 vs 16)
  B. Moser circle vs 2^(n-1): [1, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 31, 57] -> split at n=6 (31 vs 32)
  C. Proth diagonal k*2^(k-2)+1 vs companion Pell: [3, 7, 17, 41, 97, 225] vs [3, 7, 17, 41, 99, 239] -> split at 5th term (97 vs 99)
  D. MY OWN S419 MIRAGE: faulhaber shear-2 sums [1, 2, 4, 7, 12, 21, 37, 68, 129] ; law a(n)=a(n-1)+a(n-2)+a(n-4) BREAKS at m=8 (predicts 65, actual 68)
     -> S419's 'verified on all 3 instances' was a category-E jet illusion. MISTAKE-191 filed.
  E. repo's own: width of G_n = C(n-2,floor) -- exact n=3..6, fails n=7 (predicted 10, actual 15).
  MECHANISM: all five are partial low-order jets of different growth classes (quadratic /
  exponential / Pisot / radical); the shear calculus names the true class and predicts the split.

========================================================================================
(5) THE RELATIONAL SYNTHESIS: T_n = n in the relational sense
========================================================================================
  2*T_m + 1 = m^2+m+1 = Phi_6(m+1) = |PG(2,m)|: True
  deep well: 183 = 2*T_13 + 1 = 183 = |PG(2,13)| -- the LRC deep well denominator IS the doubled-relation-plus-one of 13.
  the owner's (1,n)=2n+1 column, evaluated AT triangular arguments, = projective plane sizes.
  tournament tower: #labeled tournaments on n = 2^T(n-1); staircase tile count m = C(n-1,2) = T(n-2):
    the repo's tiling hypercube Q_m has DIMENSION a triangular number -- relation count as exponent.
  centered polygonals = s*T(n-1) + 1: the bilinear m*b+1 rim WITH TRIANGULAR BASE.
  hexagonal fold-back: H(n) = T(2n-1): True (the polygonal tower folds into the triangular spine)

  CONTINUATION AXES CENSUS (the wide berth): 8 independent axes out of T_n:
   1. arity      : C(n,2) -> C(n,k)          [k-ary relations, hypergraph edges]
   2. geometry   : P(3,n) -> P(s,n)          [polygonal; hexagonal folds back to T]
   3. dimension  : C(n+1,2) -> C(n+d-1,d)    [simplicial: tetrahedral, pentatope...]
   4. exponent   : sum j^1 -> sum j^k        [Faulhaber tower, S419's third perspective]
   5. field      : C(n,2) -> [n,2]_q         [q-analog: 2-subspaces of F_q^n; q=2 the dyadic shadow]
   6. orientation: T_n -> 2^(T_n)            [tournaments; sums=elements, products=orientations]
   7. centering  : T_n -> s*T(n-1)+1         [centered polygonal = bilinear rim on base T]
   8. projective : T_n -> 2T_n+1 = |PG(2,n)| [doubling+duty: the deep well axis]
  q-check [n,2]_2 (dyadic triangulars): [1, 7, 35, 155, 651, 2667] (1,7,35,155,651,2667: 2-subspaces of F_2^n)

